1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,312
A Patient Care Record, or PCR, should let another clinician
reconstruct the encounter. It is a clinical, legal and operational
record, not merely a task to finish after transport.

2
00:00:12,312 --> 00:00:23,232
Record the patient's concern, assessment, reasoning, care, response
and outcome. Include the times and communications that explain how the
plan developed.

3
00:00:23,232 --> 00:00:37,536
The phrase if it was not documented, it did not happen is a warning
about missing evidence, not a literal rule that undocumented care
never occurred. Accurate entries are needed precisely because memory
alone is unreliable.

4
00:00:37,536 --> 00:00:49,200
Timely, clear records support the patient and the next team. Writing
mainly to defend yourself can distort priorities; record the clinical
story honestly, including uncertainty.

5
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:59,640
Begin with correct patient identification and the reason for contact.
Verify information when possible and identify its source when it comes
from someone else.

6
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:11,304
Record relevant history and examination findings, including measured
values, units and times. A finding is not negative merely because no
one asked about it.

7
00:01:11,304 --> 00:01:23,112
Link care to reasoning and reassessment. Include consent discussions,
clinical advice and the final disposition. The actual ePCR may contain
additional required fields.

8
00:01:23,112 --> 00:01:34,200
Review the whole record before finalizing it. A completed checkbox can
still be wrong or contradicted by the narrative; correct the
inconsistency using the approved process.

9
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:45,720
Objective information is observed or measured. Subjective information
includes symptoms and history reported by the patient or a witness.
Both belong in a useful record.

10
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:57,720
Objectivity means representing each source accurately, not deleting
symptoms because you cannot measure them. Patient reports severe pain
is appropriate when that is what the patient reports.

11
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:08,376
Label a clinical impression as an impression and support it with
findings. Do not turn an uncertain loss of consciousness or a
suspected injury into a confirmed event.

12
00:02:08,376 --> 00:02:17,592
Replace character judgements with specific behaviour and context.
Describe what the person declined, what was explained and what
happened next.

13
00:02:17,592 --> 00:02:29,448
Keep symptom onset, call time, assessment time, intervention time and
entry time distinct. They may differ, and that difference can change
clinical interpretation.

14
00:02:29,448 --> 00:02:40,512
Use time-linked findings to show a trend. A later reading must not be
placed beside an earlier intervention in a way that falsely suggests
it was the pretreatment baseline.

15
00:02:40,512 --> 00:02:50,760
If an onset or time is approximate, say so and identify the source. Do
not invent minute-level precision because the form has a time field.

16
00:02:50,760 --> 00:03:03,336
Brief contemporaneous notes can help, provided they are handled
securely and transferred according to policy. Urgent care takes
priority, but a later entry must remain transparent.

17
00:03:03,336 --> 00:03:12,984
An intervention without a baseline is difficult to interpret. Record
the relevant assessment before care, then the action and subsequent
findings.

18
00:03:12,984 --> 00:03:25,656
For a medication, include the full drug, dose and units, route and
administration time. Record the relevant effect and reassessment
rather than assuming the expected response occurred.

19
00:03:25,656 --> 00:03:36,816
If the intervention did not help, that is important information.
Document the lack of improvement, continued concern and any change in
management or request for assistance.

20
00:03:36,816 --> 00:03:48,528
A clinical consultation needs the person or service, time, relevant
advice and action taken. Verify instructions and ensure any action
remains within your authorization.

21
00:03:48,528 --> 00:04:00,024
If a value or time is wrong, correct it through the approved record
process. Do not silently rewrite history or copy a number into several
fields to make the record appear complete.

22
00:04:00,024 --> 00:04:11,976
A late entry should be identifiable as late and tied to the actual
event as accurately as possible. Follow local requirements rather than
inventing a universal correction format.

23
00:04:11,976 --> 00:04:20,664
If care is still ongoing and the error could change a decision,
communicate the correction directly to the relevant clinician as well
as correcting the record.

24
00:04:20,664 --> 00:04:33,744
Not obtained, declined, not indicated and unknown describe different
situations. Use the appropriate description and explain missing
required information rather than substituting a normal value.

25
00:04:33,744 --> 00:04:46,560
The BC privacy framework distinguishes public bodies under FIPPA, also
commonly called FOIPPA, from private organizations under PIPA. Do not
assume both laws apply identically to every employer.

26
00:04:46,560 --> 00:05:07,176
Protected information is not limited to a written name. Conversations,
images and device screens can identify a person. The school calls
identification through combined details mosaic identification: age,
location, time and event can identify someone even when a name is
removed.

27
00:05:07,176 --> 00:05:20,064
Before sharing, verify the recipient and the applicable authority and
purpose. Share the necessary information through an approved channel,
not simply with anyone who appears interested or helpful.

28
00:05:20,064 --> 00:05:32,976
Clinical care is an important authorized purpose, but other lawful
disclosures can exist. If the legal basis is unclear, seek the
appropriate privacy or supervisory advice rather than guessing.

29
00:05:32,976 --> 00:05:43,872
A crowded home or public scene does not remove the duty to protect
privacy. Lower your voice, reposition when feasible and avoid
displaying the record to bystanders.

30
00:05:43,872 --> 00:05:54,960
Family can provide valuable history, but being a relative does not
automatically authorize every disclosure or decision. Ask the capable
patient what they agree to share.

31
00:05:54,960 --> 00:06:05,448
Use only approved devices, storage and transmission methods. Keep
paperwork secure and follow the service's process for temporary notes
and printed records.

32
00:06:05,448 --> 00:06:16,008
During handover, give necessary information to the appropriate team
while limiting avoidable exposure. Privacy and an effective clinical
report should support each other.

33
00:06:16,008 --> 00:06:27,408
A scene photo can reveal a home, vehicle or location even when no
patient is visible. Do not use personal photography or social media to
store or share case material.

34
00:06:27,408 --> 00:06:38,904
The same boundary principle applies offline: avoid private financial
favours, excessive personal disclosure and personal contact that
shifts care into a private relationship.

35
00:06:38,904 --> 00:06:50,592
If you already know the patient, protect necessary care and manage the
conflict with appropriate support. Maintain privacy, role clarity and
the same assessment standards.

36
00:06:50,592 --> 00:07:03,960
After a difficult encounter, seek appropriate confidential support and
remove identifying details from personal reflection. Stress is a
reason to obtain help, not permission to expose a patient.

37
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:15,504
Suppose patient details are sent to the wrong approved-system
recipient. First prevent further exposure where possible, then
promptly use the organization's privacy-incident process.

38
00:07:15,504 --> 00:07:27,000
Do not hide the incident or assume it is harmless because the
recipient seems trustworthy. The privacy team needs facts to assess
the information, recipients and possible consequences.

39
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:38,664
Record the time, information involved, recipient and containment
actions through the appropriate channel. Follow instructions about
preserving records and contacting the recipient.

40
00:07:38,664 --> 00:07:50,784
Patient notification and external reporting depend on the applicable
process and legal duties. Do not invent a universal notification
deadline or promise an outcome you cannot guarantee.

41
00:07:50,784 --> 00:08:02,064
Consent is a process, not merely a signature. Explain the proposed
care and why you recommend it, along with important benefits, risks
and reasonable alternatives.

42
00:08:02,064 --> 00:08:11,400
Give the person an opportunity to ask questions. Use appropriate
language support and check what they understood, rather than simply
asking whether they agree.

43
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:24,384
Consent can be expressed verbally, in writing or through relevant
conduct, but it applies to the particular care agreed to. Permission
for one examination does not authorize every later intervention.

44
00:08:24,384 --> 00:08:34,152
Document the discussion and decision. If consent is withdrawn or the
plan changes, reassess the situation and record the new decision and
actions.

45
00:08:34,152 --> 00:08:45,984
Consent inferred from conduct can be a capable patient extending an
arm after an explained examination. It still depends on the situation
and the particular care being proposed.

46
00:08:45,984 --> 00:08:58,296
An unconscious patient presents a different issue: there may be a
lawful basis for necessary urgent care without express consent. The
word implied alone does not establish that basis.

47
00:08:58,296 --> 00:09:11,040
Known valid refusals, applicable advance instructions and the
available decision-making process must be considered. An emergency is
not automatic permission to ignore every prior decision.

48
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:23,496
Record urgency, the capability concern, what information was available
and the basis for the action. Seek clinical support where needed and
obtain the patient's decision when they can participate.

49
00:09:23,496 --> 00:09:35,880
Adult capability is presumed unless there is evidence otherwise.
Assess the actual decision at the actual time, using understandable
information and appropriate communication support.

50
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:48,096
Document how the person understands the relevant information and its
application to their own circumstances. Use their meaningful
responses, not just a statement that capacity was assessed.

51
00:09:48,096 --> 00:10:02,832
Orientation and a neurological score provide clinical context but do
not alone prove capability. An unusual choice, dementia diagnosis or
reported alcohol use also does not automatically decide the question.

52
00:10:02,832 --> 00:10:28,800
Identify barriers to understanding or voluntariness, reassess changes
and obtain support when uncertain. The school example names VIRCA; its
consent module expands this as Voluntary, Informed, Relevant, Capacity
and Advice. These prompt the discussion, the specific decision,
capability evidence and follow-up. The letters are not a validated
score or a substitute for that evidence.

53
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:40,896
Refusal of one element does not automatically mean refusal of every
aspect of care. Ask what the person is declining and why, then clarify
which assessment or support they will accept.

54
00:10:40,896 --> 00:10:51,840
Explain clinically relevant risks and uncertainty in terms the patient
can use. A generic risks explained entry does not show whether the
actual decision was informed.

55
00:10:51,840 --> 00:11:04,368
Address practical or communication barriers without coercion. Seek the
required consultation and extra help for high-risk or uncertain
situations; do not use a signature as a shortcut.

56
00:11:04,368 --> 00:11:20,088
The current non-conveyance guideline explicitly requires CliniCall
consultation for pediatric patients and patients in police custody.
Its broader mandatory-consultation list remains an operational
requirement, not something to guess from memory.

57
00:11:20,088 --> 00:11:32,304
For a refusal, show the clinical picture and why the proposed plan
mattered. Record the assessment findings, any assessment the patient
declined and how that limited your conclusions.

58
00:11:32,304 --> 00:11:41,928
Record the specific risks, benefits and alternatives discussed, the
person's responses, their decision and the evidence supporting your
capability judgement.

59
00:11:41,928 --> 00:11:52,800
Include consultations and the final safety plan. Advice must explain
when and how to obtain further help, and the record should show how
understanding was checked.

60
00:11:52,800 --> 00:12:05,736
The signature supports the record but does not replace the process. If
the patient declines to sign, record that fact and the discussion
rather than inventing a signature or abandoning the documentation.

61
00:12:05,736 --> 00:12:18,072
The BC Infants Act allows a minor to consent when the statutory
requirements are met. The provider must explain the care and be
satisfied about the minor's understanding and best interests.

62
00:12:18,072 --> 00:12:30,720
Understanding concerns the nature and consequences of care and
reasonably foreseeable benefits and risks. There is no universal
birthday that automatically establishes this for every decision.

63
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:42,960
Record whether consent came from the minor or another verified
authorized person and why that source was appropriate. Document
communication, relevant disagreements and clinical support.

64
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:53,712
Do not turn this summary into an independent pediatric release rule.
Current BCEHS non-conveyance guidance requires CliniCall consultation
for pediatric patients.

65
00:12:53,712 --> 00:13:04,776
An advance directive is a capable adult's written instruction meeting
the legal requirements. A MOST is a medical order, not simply another
name for an advance directive.

66
00:13:04,776 --> 00:13:14,232
A No CPR direction concerns resuscitation. It does not automatically
mean no assessment, no symptom relief or no other care.

67
00:13:14,232 --> 00:13:26,040
Verify whose document it is, what it directs and whether it applies to
the present situation. Record the document type, relevant content and
any required approved-system copy.

68
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:40,104
If there is uncertainty or conflict, obtain appropriate clinical
consultation rather than casually overriding the document. Detailed
resuscitation decisions require the applicable pathway, not this
glossary summary.

69
00:13:40,104 --> 00:13:51,984
A substitute decision-maker is relevant when the patient cannot make
the applicable decision. Do not bypass a capable patient simply
because a family member is easier to speak with.

70
00:13:51,984 --> 00:14:02,400
Verify the person's authority and its scope. A representation
agreement, an existing appointment and an informal family relationship
are not interchangeable.

71
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:15,456
BCEHS guidance contains details and some inconsistent wording about
identifying or appointing substitutes. Do not assume that a paramedic
can independently create an appointment by choosing a relative.

72
00:14:15,456 --> 00:14:27,432
Use the appropriate clinical and legal support when authority is
unclear. Record the document or basis checked, the person involved,
their direction and the consultation that supported the plan.

73
00:14:27,432 --> 00:14:38,880
Arrived at hospital records a location, not a completed transfer.
Identify the receiving person and role, time, relevant report and
acceptance of care.

74
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:53,016
Use the appropriate service framework. Current BCEHS A03 uses ATMIST
AMBO for transfer and SBAR for consultation, even though parts of the
school text use broader SBAR and MIST labels.

75
00:14:53,016 --> 00:15:06,336
The school also gives different expansions of ISBARR in modules 2A and
4A. Learn the required information and distinguish those labels rather
than assuming the letters have one universal expansion.

76
00:15:06,336 --> 00:15:17,256
Record outstanding issues and required documents or traces supplied.
The written record and verbal report should agree about the latest
status and the care already provided.

77
00:15:17,256 --> 00:15:28,656
A patient not being transported still had a clinical encounter. The
record needs the assessment, reasoning and actual outcome, not just a
non-transport checkbox.

78
00:15:28,656 --> 00:15:40,560
Differentiate an informed refusal from a clinically appropriate
alternative pathway. If a referral is part of the plan, verify its
availability or use the established referral process.

79
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:53,664
The refusal documentation standard calls for two complete observation
sets; if required assessment cannot be obtained, explain why.
Unobtained data must never be invented to complete the form.

80
00:15:53,664 --> 00:16:06,120
Record specific follow-up and worsening advice, who participated and
how understanding was checked. A referral offer, an accepted referral
and a completed transfer are different outcomes.

81
00:16:06,120 --> 00:16:15,984
The reading section contains a fictional timeline and an original
model narrative. It is not a school assessment answer or an official
ePCR template.

82
00:16:15,984 --> 00:16:27,048
Write the record from supplied information only. Attribute the
patient's account, preserve measurement times and describe the care
and response without diagnosing a deeper injury.

83
00:16:27,048 --> 00:16:38,976
The example has transport and a receiving nurse so that the
accountability sequence can close. A comparable non-conveyance record
would need its own decision rationale and safety plan.

84
00:16:38,976 --> 00:16:48,840
Use the example to learn the structure, then complete your school
practice on the official form. Never copy fictional values into a real
patient record.

85
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:58,752
Ask a peer or instructor to reconstruct the call from the PCR. If they
must guess why you intervened or who accepted care, the record needs
improvement.

86
00:16:58,752 --> 00:17:10,800
Also review the relationship: did you support trust and autonomy,
protect dignity, recognize bias and respond to nonverbal cues? Was the
environment or your language a barrier?

87
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:35,568
The school asks you to rate six communication competencies from one,
needs improvement, to five, mastered, and choose one skill to refine
over thirty days. Review trust, environment, bias, nonverbal cues, de-
escalation and adaptation. Support ratings with observed behaviour and
feedback: a self-rating of five does not certify mastery.

88
00:17:35,568 --> 00:17:46,968
Track feedback and a repeat performance. The record, handover and
patient interaction should improve together rather than becoming three
unrelated paperwork exercises.
